      "content": "That makes sense. So, if the standard OLS formula is calculating a variance that is smaller than the true variance, it sounds like the model is essentially being too \"confident\" in its estimates. \n\nIt's almost as if the errors are following a pattern or a hidden logic that the model isn't seeing, making the results look more stable than they actually are. Why does this pattern typically emerge in sociological time-series data? Is it just random fluctuations, or is there something inherent in how these variables interact over time that makes the errors seem to \"track\" each other?",
